1186	717	1135	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	SQL Server's auto-update statistics threshold is 20% + 500 rows (or SQRT(1000 * rows) with TF 2371 / SQL 2016+ auto). For large tables, this means millions of rows can change before an update triggers. Stale statistics cause the optimizer to estimate wrong row counts, choosing hash joins over nested loops, scans over seeks, or allocating wrong memory grants (causing spills). sys.dm_db_stats_properties exposes modification_counter which tracks DML changes since last stats update. Fix: (1) run UPDATE STATISTICS on high-modification tables with FULLSCAN or a high sample rate, (2) enable auto-update statistics asynchronously (AUTO_UPDATE_STATISTICS_ASYNC) to avoid blocking, (3) create a maintenance job for statistics updates on large tables, (4) consider incremental statistics on partitioned tables.
