1188	718	1137	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	Cardinality estimation (CE) is the foundation of query optimization -- wrong row estimates cascade through the plan, affecting join strategies, memory grants, and parallelism decisions. Common CE issues: (1) correlated columns where independent selectivity assumption fails (use multi-column statistics), (2) ascending key problem where new values exceed histogram range, (3) table variables always estimated at 1 row (use temp tables or OPTION(RECOMPILE)), (4) implicit conversions preventing histogram use. SQL Server has two CE models: Legacy CE (compat level <120) and New CE (120+). Fix: (1) create multi-column statistics for correlated predicates, (2) update statistics with FULLSCAN on key tables, (3) use trace flag 4199 for optimizer fixes, (4) use OPTION(RECOMPILE) for parameter-sensitive queries, (5) force good plans via Query Store.
