1190	719	1139	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	SQL Server's equivalent of PostgreSQL's dead tuples are ghost records -- rows marked for deletion that the Ghost Cleanup task removes asynchronously. When ghost records accumulate (e.g., under RCSI or high delete workloads), scans must skip them, wasting CPU and buffer reads. Index fragmentation means pages are out of order on disk (external) or under-filled (internal), both of which increase the number of pages needed to store and scan the same data. Forwarded records in heap tables cause extra I/O as the storage engine follows forwarding pointers. Fix: (1) rebuild indexes with > 30% fragmentation (ALTER INDEX REBUILD), (2) reorganize indexes with 10-30% fragmentation, (3) monitor ghost cleanup with trace flag 661, (4) convert heaps to clustered indexes to eliminate forwarding, (5) schedule regular index maintenance (Ola Hallengren scripts).
