1192	720	1141	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	SQL Server checkpoint writes all dirty pages for a database to disk, ensuring crash recovery stays within the configured recovery interval. The default recovery interval is 0 (auto, approximately 1 minute target recovery time). Indirect checkpoints (default in SQL Server 2016+ for new databases, configured via TARGET_RECOVERY_TIME) provide smoother I/O but can be more aggressive. When checkpoint runs too frequently, it flushes dirty pages that are still being actively modified, causing them to be re-dirtied and re-written, wasting both I/O bandwidth and CPU. Fix: (1) increase recovery interval if crash recovery time is acceptable (sp_configure 'recovery interval', 300), (2) for indirect checkpoints, increase TARGET_RECOVERY_TIME (ALTER DATABASE SET TARGET_RECOVERY_TIME = 120 SECONDS), (3) ensure I/O subsystem can handle the write volume, (4) consider separating log and data files to different storage.
