1194	721	1143	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	Memory overcommitment occurs when the total memory demanded by all processes exceeds physical RAM, causing the OS to page memory to the swap file. When SQL Server buffer pool pages are paged out, subsequent access requires reading from swap (much slower than RAM), negating the purpose of caching. sys.dm_os_sys_memory.system_memory_state_desc shows: 'Available physical memory is high' (healthy), 'Physical memory usage is steady', 'Available physical memory is low' (problem), 'Available physical memory is running low' (critical). sys.dm_os_process_memory.process_physical_memory_low = 1 means SQL Server received a low memory notification. Fix: (1) reduce max server memory to leave adequate OS headroom (10-20%), (2) stop competing memory-intensive services, (3) enable Lock Pages in Memory (LPIM) for the SQL Server service account to prevent the OS from paging out the buffer pool, (4) add physical RAM.
