1196	722	1145	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	Ad-hoc queries with embedded literal values (e.g., WHERE id = 42, WHERE id = 43) each get their own plan cache entry because SQL Server treats them as distinct queries. query_hash normalizes literals, so plans with the same query_hash but different usecounts=1 entries are the same logical query with different constants. This wastes plan cache memory and can cause the cache to evict useful plans under memory pressure. Fix: (1) enable 'optimize for ad hoc workloads' (sp_configure) which caches only a plan stub on first execution, (2) enable forced parameterization (ALTER DATABASE SET PARAMETERIZATION FORCED) for workloads that cannot be modified, (3) use sp_executesql with parameters in application code, (4) use prepared statements in client drivers.
