1198	723	1147	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	SQL Server computes sql_handle from the exact query text bytes (including whitespace, casing, and comments), while query_hash normalizes these differences. So 'SELECT * FROM t WHERE id=1' and 'select * from T where ID = 1' produce different sql_handle values but the same query_hash. Each variant gets its own plan cache entry. This is common when: (1) different ORM versions generate slightly different formatting, (2) developers write ad-hoc queries with inconsistent style, (3) generated SQL uses varying amounts of whitespace or comment blocks. Fix: (1) standardize query generation in application code, (2) use stored procedures or parameterized queries that produce consistent text, (3) enable 'optimize for ad hoc workloads' to reduce memory waste from single-use stubs, (4) periodically run DBCC FREEPROCCACHE to reset bloated cache (only during low-activity periods).
