1202	725	1151	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	Dynamic SQL built with string concatenation (EXEC('SELECT * FROM t WHERE id=' + @id)) creates a new plan for each unique string, while sp_executesql with parameters (EXEC sp_executesql N'SELECT * FROM t WHERE id=@p', N'@p int', @p=42) reuses plans across executions. The difference is critical for plan cache efficiency. EXEC() treats the entire string as ad-hoc SQL, while sp_executesql enables parameterized plan reuse. Common sources: (1) legacy stored procedures using EXEC(), (2) ORM-generated queries without parameter binding, (3) reporting tools that concatenate filter values, (4) search screens with optional criteria built via string concatenation. Fix: (1) replace EXEC() with sp_executesql and parameter bindings, (2) configure ORM/driver to use parameterized queries, (3) use OPTION(RECOMPILE) for complex dynamic search patterns, (4) enable forced parameterization as a server-wide safety net.
1203	725	1152	2	confirmed	\N	ruled_out	\N	Dynamic SQL built with string concatenation (EXEC('SELECT * FROM t WHERE id=' + @id)) creates a new plan for each unique string, while sp_executesql with parameters (EXEC sp_executesql N'SELECT * FROM t WHERE id=@p', N'@p int', @p=42) reuses plans across executions. The difference is critical for plan cache efficiency. EXEC() treats the entire string as ad-hoc SQL, while sp_executesql enables parameterized plan reuse. Common sources: (1) legacy stored procedures using EXEC(), (2) ORM-generated queries without parameter binding, (3) reporting tools that concatenate filter values, (4) search screens with optional criteria built via string concatenation. Fix: (1) replace EXEC() with sp_executesql and parameter bindings, (2) configure ORM/driver to use parameterized queries, (3) use OPTION(RECOMPILE) for complex dynamic search patterns, (4) enable forced parameterization as a server-wide safety net.
1204	726	1153	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	When multiple applications (web app, reporting tool, ETL jobs, monitoring agents) connect to the same database, each may generate logically identical queries with different formatting, parameter styles, or SET options. Since plan cache keys include the exact query text and SET options, each variant gets its own entry. Common culprits: (1) Java/JDBC vs .NET/ADO.NET generate different parameter styles, (2) reporting tools embed literal values, (3) monitoring agents run ad-hoc queries every polling interval, (4) different ORM versions generate slightly different SQL. Fix: (1) standardize data access layers across applications, (2) use stored procedures as a shared API layer, (3) enable 'optimize for ad hoc workloads', (4) configure all applications to use the same SET options (ANSI_NULLS, QUOTED_IDENTIFIER, etc.), (5) consider forced parameterization for the database.
