1206	727	1155	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	SQL Server's simple parameterization (default) only auto-parameterizes trivially simple queries: single-table SELECT/INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE with equality predicates on constants. It fails for: (1) queries with JOINs, (2) IN clauses, (3) LIKE predicates, (4) queries with UNION/INTERSECT/EXCEPT, (5) queries referencing views, (6) queries with subqueries, (7) queries with TOP, (8) aggregate queries. When simple parameterization fails, each unique set of literal values creates a new plan cache entry. The fix depends on the situation: (1) forced parameterization (ALTER DATABASE SET PARAMETERIZATION FORCED) handles most cases but can cause parameter sniffing issues, (2) plan guides can force parameterization for specific query patterns, (3) sp_executesql with explicit parameters in application code is the gold standard, (4) 'optimize for ad hoc workloads' reduces memory waste by caching only stubs on first use.
1207	727	1156	2	confirmed	\N	ruled_out	\N	SQL Server's simple parameterization (default) only auto-parameterizes trivially simple queries: single-table SELECT/INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE with equality predicates on constants. It fails for: (1) queries with JOINs, (2) IN clauses, (3) LIKE predicates, (4) queries with UNION/INTERSECT/EXCEPT, (5) queries referencing views, (6) queries with subqueries, (7) queries with TOP, (8) aggregate queries. When simple parameterization fails, each unique set of literal values creates a new plan cache entry. The fix depends on the situation: (1) forced parameterization (ALTER DATABASE SET PARAMETERIZATION FORCED) handles most cases but can cause parameter sniffing issues, (2) plan guides can force parameterization for specific query patterns, (3) sp_executesql with explicit parameters in application code is the gold standard, (4) 'optimize for ad hoc workloads' reduces memory waste by caching only stubs on first use.
1208	728	1157	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	Forced parameterization (ALTER DATABASE SET PARAMETERIZATION FORCED) makes SQL Server parameterize all queries except those with query hints. This is beneficial for OLTP workloads with many ad-hoc queries generating plan cache bloat, but harmful when the workload has parameter-sensitive queries (different parameter values need different plans). Symptoms: performance regression on queries with skewed data distributions. Fix: switch back to SIMPLE parameterization and use plan guides or OPTIMIZE FOR hints on specific queries, or use Query Store forced plans.
