1210	729	1159	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	When 'optimize for ad hoc workloads' is disabled (default), SQL Server caches the full compiled plan on first execution. For workloads with many unique queries (e.g., ORM-generated, dynamic SQL), this wastes plan cache memory. Step 2 confirms even if the setting is already enabled — the symptom (high single-use ratio) is the root cause regardless. Fix: (1) enable 'optimize for ad hoc workloads' to cache only a stub on first use, (2) parameterize application queries, (3) use sp_executesql instead of dynamic SQL. DBCC FREEPROCCACHE clears accumulated bloat but causes a temporary performance dip as plans are recompiled.
