1264	753	1212	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	Random UUID v4 (NEWID()) as a clustered index key is one of the worst patterns in SQL Server. Every INSERT causes a mid-page split because the random value lands between existing rows. This causes: (1) 50% page density (each split creates two half-full pages), (2) extreme logical fragmentation, (3) excessive I/O for sequential scans, (4) increased transaction log volume from page split operations. Fix: replace NEWID() with NEWSEQUENTIALID() for sequential GUIDs, switch to IDENTITY(1,1) or SEQUENCE for the clustered key and keep the GUID as a non-clustered unique constraint, or use a composite key with a sequential leading column. If GUIDs are required for distributed systems, consider NEWSEQUENTIALID() which is monotonic within a server.
