1297	768	1245	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	Connection leaks are application-side bugs where the code acquires a connection from the pool (e.g., SqlConnection.Open()) but fails to call .Close() or .Dispose(). The connection remains allocated from the pool's perspective but is orphaned in application memory. Over time, the pool fills with leaked connections, and new requests get pool timeout exceptions. SQL Server sees these as sleeping sessions with no open transaction and no recent activity. Detection is indirect because SQL Server cannot distinguish a legitimate idle pooled connection from a leaked one. The pattern to look for is: same program_name, growing count of idle sessions over time, high max_idle_minutes. Fix: (1) review application code for missing connection disposal, (2) use 'using' blocks in C# or try-with-resources in Java, (3) set connection lifetime in pool settings to force periodic recycling, (4) enable application-side pool monitoring and alerting.
