1373	803	1321	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	ORM frameworks like Entity Framework and NHibernate implement lazy-loading by deferring related entity queries until accessed. When the ORM does not hold a long-lived connection or DbContext, each lazy-load navigation triggers: (1) acquire connection from pool, (2) execute SELECT ... WHERE FK = @id, (3) release connection back to pool. For a parent entity with 100 child records, this creates 100 separate connection acquire/release cycles. The connection pool handles this efficiently if pool min size is sufficient, but the overhead of sp_reset_connection for each reset adds up. Fix: use eager loading (.Include() in EF Core), batch lazy loads (EF Core filtered includes), or switch to explicit loading with a single connection scope. Increase min pool size to avoid cold-start allocation.
