1379	805	1327	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	SQL Server's default 'user connections' setting is 0 (unlimited, dynamically allocated up to 32,767). The practical limit is usually worker threads (default: 512 for most systems). When all worker threads are consumed, new connection attempts queue on the THREADPOOL wait, causing timeouts and application errors. Unlike PostgreSQL's hard max_connections, SQL Server's limit is more about worker thread availability than connection count. Fix: identify and reduce connection pool sizes in individual applications (they often default to 100), implement connection pooling middleware, close idle connections aggressively, and consider increasing max worker threads if CPU count supports it.
