1385	807	1333	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	In microservice architectures, each service instance typically creates its own connection pool with default settings (e.g., MaxPoolSize=100 in ADO.NET, maximumPoolSize=10 in HikariCP). With 20 microservice types and 3 instances each, that's potentially 60 pools x 100 max = 6,000 maximum connections, far beyond what most SQL Server instances can handle. Most of these connections sit idle. Fix: reduce MaxPoolSize per service to match actual concurrency needs (often 5-10 is sufficient), use a connection proxy like ProxySQL or PgBouncer equivalent for SQL Server, consolidate database access through a data access service, and monitor aggregate connection counts with alerts at 70% capacity.
