1388	808	1336	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	In SQL Server, 'user connections' is a dynamic configuration option (takes effect immediately via sp_configure + RECONFIGURE), but 'max worker threads' requires a restart to take effect. The sys.configurations view shows both the configured value (value column — what was set) and the running value (value_in_use column — what is actually active). When an administrator increases max worker threads but forgets to restart, the server continues running with the old limit. Fix: schedule a maintenance window to restart SQL Server so the new max worker threads takes effect. Note that increasing max worker threads beyond the default is rarely needed and can cause excessive context switching — ensure the increase is justified by actual THREADPOOL waits.
