1403	813	1351	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	Batch and ETL processes (SSIS packages, BCP operations, SQL Agent jobs, custom import scripts) often open connections early and hold them for the entire job duration. When a batch process hangs (due to blocking, deadlocks, network issues, or application logic errors), it continues holding the connection indefinitely. Multiple hung batch connections can consume significant capacity. Particularly dangerous are sessions with open_transaction_count > 0 while sleeping — these hold locks and consume log space. Fix: implement query timeouts for batch connections (CommandTimeout in ADO.NET, queryTimeout in JDBC), set up SQL Agent alerts for long-running jobs, use KILL command for confirmed hung sessions, add connection timeout to ETL connection strings, and implement watchdog queries to detect and alert on stuck processes.
