1412	816	1360	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	Each SQL Server connection requires memory for: session context (~380 KB), network input/output buffers (~32 KB each), security token (~8 KB), and associated memory objects. Under memory pressure, SQL Server cannot allocate these structures and will reject new connections with error 701 (insufficient system memory) or error 17809 (could not connect because maximum number of user connections already reached). This is separate from worker thread exhaustion — the server may have available worker threads but no memory to initialize the connection. Fix: increase SQL Server max server memory if the host has available RAM, identify and reduce memory consumers (oversized query memory grants, excessive plan cache), reduce the number of idle connections, and investigate memory leaks from linked server queries or CLR objects.
