1499	853	1447	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	SQL Server's query optimizer uses a cost-based search with a time budget proportional to the estimated query cost. For complex queries (10+ joins, nested subqueries, many CTEs), the optimizer may exhaust its time budget and abort with 'TimeOut' — producing a plan that may be far from optimal. Each recompilation of such a query wastes 50-500ms+ of CPU. Fix: (1) simplify the query by breaking it into temp table stages, (2) use indexed views to pre-join frequently used combinations, (3) add OPTION(FORCE ORDER) if the optimal join order is known, (4) use plan guides to freeze a known-good plan, (5) reduce the number of joins by denormalizing or using indexed views.
