1501	854	1449	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	SQL Server encodes certain session settings (including isolation level implications via SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL) into the plan cache key. When the same stored procedure is called from sessions using READ COMMITTED and then from sessions using SERIALIZABLE, separate plans are compiled and cached. This multiplies plan cache consumption and compilation overhead. Additionally, SERIALIZABLE may cause the optimizer to choose different access paths (range locks, key-range scans) than READ COMMITTED. Fix: (1) standardize isolation level across application connections, (2) use table hints (WITH (NOLOCK), WITH (SERIALIZABLE)) for specific statements rather than session-level SET, (3) consider READ COMMITTED SNAPSHOT ISOLATION (RCSI) as a unified approach.
