1503	855	1451	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	Non-deterministic scalar UDFs in SQL Server are a well-known performance anti-pattern. Before SQL Server 2019, ALL scalar UDFs forced per-row evaluation (cursor-like behavior) because the optimizer could not inline them into the query plan. SQL Server 2019+ (compatibility level 150+) can inline deterministic scalar UDFs, but non-deterministic ones (using GETDATE, NEWID, RAND, or data-accessing) remain non-inlineable. Each call forces context switching between the query and the function. Fix: (1) replace scalar UDFs with inline table-valued functions (iTVFs), (2) move GETDATE() calls to a variable assignment before the query, (3) use computed columns for deterministic calculations, (4) upgrade to SQL 2019+ and compat level 150 for automatic inlining of eligible UDFs.
