1529	867	1477	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	Cursors in SQL Server process rows one at a time, preventing the query optimizer from choosing efficient set-based plans. Each FETCH NEXT is essentially a separate query execution. A cursor processing 1M rows makes 1M round-trips to the storage engine. Common cursor anti-patterns: (1) WHILE loops with TOP 1 queries, (2) CURSOR FOR SELECT with FETCH NEXT and INSERT, (3) running totals calculated row-by-row. Fix: (1) rewrite using set-based operations (JOINs, CTEs, window functions), (2) use MERGE for upsert patterns, (3) use window functions (ROW_NUMBER, LAG/LEAD, running SUM) instead of cursor-based calculations. If a cursor is truly necessary, use FAST_FORWARD (read-only, forward-only) for minimal overhead.
