1538	871	1486	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	SQL Server memory pressure manifests in three ways: (1) internal — buffer pool cannot keep enough data pages cached (PLE drops), (2) external — OS is running low on physical memory (may signal SQL Server to release pages), (3) query workspace — queries cannot get memory grants for sort/hash operations. Key metrics: PLE < 300s per NUMA node is concerning (scale with RAM: ~300s per 4GB). Lazy writes/sec > 0 means buffer pool is actively evicting dirty pages — the lazy writer should be mostly idle. Fix: (1) increase max server memory, (2) reduce memory pressure from other processes, (3) optimize queries to reduce memory grant requirements, (4) enable Lock Pages in Memory (LPIM) to prevent Windows paging SQL Server memory.
