1543	873	1491	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	Ghost records are SQL Server's equivalent of PostgreSQL dead tuples. When rows are deleted or updated, the old version becomes a ghost record, marked for cleanup by the ghost cleanup task (background process). Unlike PostgreSQL's VACUUM, SQL Server's ghost cleanup runs automatically and cannot be manually triggered. Forwarded records occur only in heaps (tables without a clustered index): when a row update increases its size beyond the original page, SQL Server moves it and leaves a forwarding pointer. Fix for ghost record buildup: (1) ensure no long-running transactions holding cleanup, (2) check for version store pressure (RCSI/snapshot isolation). Fix for forwarded records: (1) create a clustered index (converts heap to B-tree), (2) ALTER TABLE REBUILD to compact the heap.
