1661	925	1608	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	Multi-column statistics are essential when WHERE clauses combine correlated columns (e.g., city AND state, category AND subcategory). Without them, the optimizer assumes column independence and multiplies selectivities, often underestimating cardinality dramatically. When multi-column stats go stale: (1) correlation patterns may have changed (new product categories, new regions), (2) the density vector becomes inaccurate, (3) queries get wrong join orders and join types. Auto-update tracks modifications against the first column only. Fix: (1) UPDATE STATISTICS on specific multi-column stats after ETL or data changes, (2) create multi-column statistics for frequently co-filtered columns, (3) use SQL Server 2014+ new CE which handles correlation better with base containment assumption.
