1663	926	1610	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	When a table has significant DELETE operations (purging old data, archival), the histogram retains range_high_key values for deleted ranges. Queries with predicates in those ranges get cardinality overestimates (optimizer expects rows that no longer exist). Effects: (1) excessive memory grants (hash/sort sized for phantom rows), (2) wrong join type (hash join instead of nested loop), (3) wrong join order. The memory grant overestimation is a telltale sign — grant_utilization_pct < 20% means the optimizer expected 5x more rows than it found. Fix: UPDATE STATISTICS WITH FULLSCAN after significant delete operations. For partitioned tables, UPDATE STATISTICS on individual partitions (if incremental stats enabled).
