1783	975	1729	2	confirmed	\N	ruled_out	\N	INDEX hints lock the optimizer to a specific index that may have been optimal when written but is now suboptimal due to schema changes, data growth, or new indexes. FORCESEEK/FORCESCAN override the seek-vs-scan decision. These hints survive plan recompilation — the optimizer cannot adapt. Review: remove hint, capture both plans, compare. SQL Server 2016+ plan guides can centrally manage hints without code changes.
1665	927	1612	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	SQL Server statistics are tied to the leading column(s) of an index or manually created statistics. For a composite index (A, B, C): (1) statistics have histogram only on column A, (2) density vector for (A,B) and (A,B,C) combinations, (3) no histogram for B or C alone. When a query filters on B alone, the optimizer uses the density vector (average selectivity) instead of a histogram, which assumes uniform distribution. This causes incorrect cardinality estimates for skewed data. Fix: (1) CREATE STATISTICS on individual columns used in predicates, (2) enable AUTO_CREATE_STATISTICS (default ON), (3) for composite index predicates not starting with the leading key, create statistics with the predicate column as the first column.
