1684	934	1631	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	In SQL Server, the lazy writer is the background process that evicts least-recently-used pages from the buffer pool when free memory is low. Unlike PostgreSQL's background writer, the lazy writer only activates under memory pressure (low PLE). If the lazy writer can't free pages fast enough, free list stalls occur — queries must wait for a free buffer page. High dirty_ratio combined with free list stalls means the system is not spreading writes effectively. Fix: increase max server memory if PLE is low, add RAM, or ensure indirect checkpoint is enabled (target_recovery_time = 60) to flush dirty pages proactively.
