1686	935	1633	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	SQL Server has three background flush mechanisms: (1) Lazy writer — evicts LRU pages under memory pressure, (2) Checkpoint worker — flushes dirty pages for recovery, (3) Background writer (indirect checkpoint) — continuous flushing to meet recovery target. If lazy_writes_sec = 0, it means PLE is high (no memory pressure), which is normal. But if bg_writer_pages_sec is also 0, indirect checkpoint may not be enabled (databases still using automatic checkpoint). If all three are zero while dirty pages accumulate, the system relies entirely on periodic checkpoint — which creates I/O storms. Fix: enable indirect checkpoint on all user databases (ALTER DATABASE SET TARGET_RECOVERY_TIME = 60 SECONDS).
