1688	936	1635	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	SQL Server's equivalent to PostgreSQL's full_page_writes is the page verification mechanism. TORN_PAGE_DETECTION writes a bit pattern before each 512-byte sector of a page — detects torn writes but adds minor overhead. CHECKSUM is the modern default (SQL 2005+) and is the recommended setting — it protects against both torn writes and in-memory corruption with a single checksum per page. NONE provides no protection. Unlike PostgreSQL where full_page_writes during checkpoint is the concern, SQL Server's page verification is per-write, not per-checkpoint. Fix: ALTER DATABASE SET PAGE_VERIFY CHECKSUM. This is a best practice even if the performance difference is small, because it provides superior data integrity protection.
