1697	940	1644	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	During checkpoint, SQL Server acquires exclusive latches (PAGEIOLATCH_EX) on buffer pages being written to disk. If foreground queries need to read or modify these same pages, they must wait (PAGELATCH_SH or PAGELATCH_UP). This is most visible during large checkpoint operations on hot pages. Indirect checkpoint mitigates this by spreading writes continuously rather than in bursts. High signal_wait_time_ms within latch waits indicates CPU scheduling delays compounding the contention. Fix: enable indirect checkpoint, use faster I/O subsystem, distribute hot tables across multiple filegroups.
