1699	941	1646	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	SQL Server's TARGET_RECOVERY_TIME controls how aggressively the indirect checkpoint flushes dirty pages. Lower values = faster crash recovery but higher continuous I/O overhead. The default for new databases in SQL Server 2016+ is 60 seconds. Values below 20 seconds force the background writer to flush pages at a very high rate, which competes with foreground I/O. This is the SQL Server equivalent of tuning checkpoint_completion_target in PostgreSQL. Fix: ALTER DATABASE [dbname] SET TARGET_RECOVERY_TIME = 60 SECONDS. Only reduce below 60 if you have specific RTO requirements AND the I/O subsystem can handle it.
