1701	942	1648	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	Automatic checkpoint (target_recovery_time = 0) in SQL Server triggers based on the recovery interval server setting (default: log record count heuristic). When it runs, it must flush all dirty pages accumulated since the last checkpoint — a batch operation that can produce large I/O bursts. Indirect checkpoint (target_recovery_time > 0) continuously flushes dirty pages in the background, preventing bursts. SQL Server 2016+ defaults new databases to target_recovery_time = 60, but databases upgraded from older versions may still have target_recovery_time = 0. Fix: ALTER DATABASE [dbname] SET TARGET_RECOVERY_TIME = 60 SECONDS for all user databases.
