1723	951	1670	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	SQL Server uses proportional fill to distribute allocations across data files in a filegroup. Files with more free space receive proportionally more allocations. If tempdb data files are different sizes (e.g., initial file 8 GB, added files 1 GB), the largest file gets most of the allocations, defeating the purpose of multiple files. This recreates single-file contention on allocation pages (PFS, GAM, SGAM) of the largest file. Fix: resize all tempdb files to the same size: ALTER DATABASE tempdb MODIFY FILE (NAME=tempdev1, SIZE=8GB). Use the same FILEGROWTH setting for all files. Best practice: set initial size large enough to avoid autogrowth, with equal FILEGROWTH increments.
