1728	953	1675	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	Global temporary tables (##tablename) are visible to all sessions and persist until the creating session disconnects AND all referencing sessions are done. Unlike local temp tables (#tablename), they cannot be cached by SQL Server's temp table caching mechanism. They create cross-session contention because multiple sessions may read/write them concurrently, causing lock escalation in tempdb. They also prevent accurate tempdb space planning because their lifetime is unpredictable. Fix: replace ##temp tables with (1) local #temp tables where possible, (2) permanent tables in a scratch database with session-aware cleanup, (3) table-valued parameters for passing data between procedures, (4) memory-optimized temp tables (In-Memory OLTP schema-only tables) for high-concurrency scenarios. Step 3 on_no_match is still confirmed because the existence of large global temp tables (step 1 matched) is problematic even without immediate space pressure.
