2073	1099	2016	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	Heap tables (tables without a clustered index) store rows in no particular order. When a row is updated and the new version is larger than the original, SQL Server moves the row to a new page and leaves a forwarding pointer. Scans must then follow these pointers (extra I/O per forwarded row), and each additional page access requires a latch. Under high DML, heaps accumulate thousands of forwarded records. Fix: (1) add a clustered index to convert the heap to a B-tree (preferred), (2) rebuild the heap with ALTER TABLE ... REBUILD to eliminate forwarded records (temporary fix, they will return), (3) use page compression to reduce row sizes and prevent forwarding. The Forwarded Records/sec performance counter also tracks this in real time.
