2085	1104	2028	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	Idle-in-transaction is extremely common in applications that fetch data within a transaction, process it in application code (business logic, API calls, user interaction), then issue the next SQL command. During the idle period, all locks from previous statements in the transaction are held, blocking other sessions. This pattern is often invisible in database monitoring because the session is not actively executing. Fix: (1) minimize transaction scope — start transaction just before the first write, commit immediately after the last write, (2) move application processing outside the transaction, (3) use READ COMMITTED SNAPSHOT isolation to prevent readers from being blocked, (4) set session-level lock_timeout to prevent indefinite blocking, (5) implement connection-level idle transaction detection and automatic rollback.
