2091	1107	2034	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	Connection pooling (ADO.NET, JDBC, ODBC) reuses physical connections across application requests. When an application begins a transaction but fails to commit or rollback before returning the connection to the pool (e.g., due to an exception path that skips the commit), the next request using that connection inherits the open transaction. sp_reset_connection (automatically called by ADO.NET on pool return) rolls back open transactions, but some drivers or configurations may not call it. Fix: (1) always use try/finally or using blocks to ensure transaction commit/rollback, (2) verify the connection pool driver calls sp_reset_connection, (3) set 'connection reset=true' in the connection string (default in modern ADO.NET), (4) implement application-level middleware that checks and rolls back open transactions on request completion.
