2095	1109	2038	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	Cursor-based processing within transactions is a common anti-pattern where developers use DECLARE CURSOR / FETCH NEXT loops to process rows one-by-one while holding a transaction open. Each fetched row acquires a lock under the transaction's isolation level, and none are released until COMMIT. For a cursor over 10,000 rows, this means 10,000+ row locks held simultaneously, often escalating to page or table locks. Fix: (1) replace the cursor loop with a set-based UPDATE/INSERT/DELETE statement (most effective), (2) if row-by-row processing is required, use FAST_FORWARD cursors and process in batches with intermediate COMMITs, (3) use READ_ONLY cursors when the transaction does not modify fetched rows, (4) consider using WHILE loops with TOP N batching as an alternative to cursors, (5) if using Entity Framework or ORMs, check for N+1 query patterns that translate to cursor-like behavior.
