2149	1134	2091	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	Implicit type conversions occur when comparing varchar to nvarchar, int to varchar, datetime to varchar, etc. The optimizer cannot step through the histogram when a CONVERT function wraps the column reference, so it falls back to a 30% selectivity guess for inequality or 10% for equality. For a 10M-row table, a 30% guess means 3M estimated rows and a proportionally massive memory grant, when the actual result might be 100 rows. Fix: (1) fix the application query to use matching types (use N'' prefix for nvarchar parameters), (2) change column types to match the parameter types, (3) add explicit CAST/CONVERT in the application to match the column type, (4) check ORM-generated queries for type mismatches (Entity Framework frequently generates nvarchar parameters against varchar columns).
