2153	1136	2095	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	Correlated subqueries (WHERE EXISTS (SELECT ... WHERE inner.col = outer.col)) force the optimizer to either decorrelate into a join (efficient) or use spool operators to cache intermediate results (expensive). When decorrelation fails — common with complex predicates, multiple correlations, or NOT EXISTS — the spool approach can consume massive memory. Fix: (1) manually rewrite correlated subqueries as JOINs, (2) replace NOT EXISTS with LEFT JOIN / IS NULL pattern, (3) use temp tables to pre-materialize subquery results, (4) add indexes on the correlated columns to help the optimizer choose nested loop with index seek over hash spool, (5) consider APPLY operator as a more predictable alternative to correlated subqueries.
