2160	1139	2102	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	The SQL Server optimizer calculates sort and hash memory grants using the estimated row count multiplied by the estimated row width. For variable-length columns, it uses the maximum declared length (varchar(8000) = 8000 bytes) when no statistics or histogram data indicates average row length. A table with 5 varchar(8000) columns would be estimated at 40 KB per row for sort purposes, even if actual average row width is 200 bytes. Fix: (1) use appropriate column widths — varchar(100) instead of varchar(8000) when data never exceeds 100 chars, (2) select only needed columns (avoid SELECT *), (3) use computed columns with defined output width for sort/join keys, (4) on SQL Server 2019+, enable Memory Grant Feedback to auto-adjust based on actual usage, (5) use OPTION(MIN_GRANT_PERCENT = 0, MAX_GRANT_PERCENT = N) to bound grants.
