2168	1143	2110	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	A non-covering index causes a two-phase access pattern: (1) seek/scan the nonclustered index, (2) key lookup to the clustered index for each row to get missing columns. The key lookup destroys the index order (random access to the clustered index), so the optimizer adds a sort to reestablish ORDER BY order. This sort requires a memory grant proportional to the result set. A covering index (with INCLUDE columns) eliminates both the key lookup and the sort. Fix: (1) add INCLUDE columns to existing indexes for the SELECT columns, (2) create new covering indexes with key columns matching WHERE/JOIN/ORDER BY and INCLUDE for remaining SELECT columns, (3) balance index coverage against write overhead — adding many INCLUDE columns increases DML cost, (4) use filtered indexes for queries that always have the same WHERE clause predicate.
