2173	1145	2115	1	next	\N	next	\N	SQL Server controls memory grants through two mechanisms: (1) Resource Governor's request_max_memory_grant_percent (default 25% of pool), which caps the maximum grant any single query can receive, and (2) query hints MIN_GRANT_PERCENT / MAX_GRANT_PERCENT (SQL Server 2016+). Misconfiguration occurs when: the default 25% is too large for OLTP workloads (one query holds 25% of workspace), too small for analytical queries (large sorts always spill), or when MIN_GRANT_PERCENT forces a minimum grant that wastes memory. Fix: (1) set request_max_memory_grant_percent to 10-15% for OLTP workload groups and 25-50% for reporting groups, (2) use OPTION(MIN_GRANT_PERCENT = 0, MAX_GRANT_PERCENT = N) per query for known patterns, (3) on SQL Server 2019+, enable Memory Grant Feedback to auto-tune, (4) monitor RESOURCE_SEMAPHORE waits and adjust the total workspace memory via 'max server memory'.
