2179	1147	2121	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	The 'max server memory' setting controls the total memory SQL Server can use (buffer pool + query workspace + plan cache + other memory consumers). The recommended formula for a dedicated SQL Server: max server memory = physical RAM - OS reservation (4 GB for up to 16 GB RAM, 10% for larger). Leaving it at the default (2147483647) lets SQL Server take all available RAM, which can starve the OS, backup agents, and monitoring tools. Setting it too low wastes available RAM and forces disk I/O. Fix: (1) configure max server memory using sp_configure: set to 80-90% of physical RAM on a dedicated server, (2) enable lock pages in memory (LPIM) to prevent OS memory page-out, (3) on shared servers, coordinate with other applications and set appropriately, (4) monitor committed_kb vs committed_target_kb — if committed equals target, SQL Server is at its maximum and may benefit from a higher setting.
