2182	1148	2124	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	Even with properly configured max server memory, the buffer pool hit ratio will be low if the actively queried dataset exceeds memory. This is different from RC01 (insufficient RAM) and RC02 (undersized config) — here the server has adequate memory but the workload simply does not fit. Common scenarios: reporting queries scanning entire large tables, OLTP on databases much larger than RAM, mixed workloads where analytical queries flush the OLTP working set. Fix: (1) add indexes to convert table scans into index seeks (reduces working set from full table to index pages), (2) partition large tables and use partition elimination to limit scans, (3) archive historical data to reduce active table sizes, (4) use Resource Governor to limit memory-intensive queries to a separate pool, (5) implement table compression (PAGE or ROW) to fit more data in the same buffer pool, (6) use filtered indexes for common query predicates, (7) consider In-Memory OLTP (memory-optimized tables) for hot tables.
