2192	1152	2133	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	Heavy DML creates dirty pages that must be written to disk during checkpoints. The cycle: (1) DML modifies pages in buffer pool, (2) checkpoint flushes dirty pages to disk, (3) new DML dirtifies pages again. Under extreme DML: buffer pool becomes dominated by dirty pages, reducing space for clean cached data. Indirect checkpoint (default since SQL 2016, target recovery interval 60s) smooths checkpoint writes but doesn't eliminate the churn. WRITELOG waits increase when transaction log can't keep up. Fix: batch DML operations, reduce index count on high-write tables, consider delayed durability for throughput-critical scenarios.
