2194	1153	2135	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	Common data type waste patterns in SQL Server: (1) NVARCHAR (2 bytes/char) when VARCHAR (1 byte/char) suffices for ASCII data — doubles buffer pool consumption. (2) BIGINT (8 bytes) for columns that never exceed INT range (4 bytes). (3) UNIQUEIDENTIFIER as clustered key (16 bytes) causes wide indexes and page splits. (4) VARCHAR(MAX)/NVARCHAR(MAX) for columns that could use fixed-length types — causes off-row storage and extra LOB page reads. Buffer pool impact: wider rows = fewer rows per 8KB page = more pages needed = larger buffer pool footprint. Fix requires schema changes — ALTER TABLE ALTER COLUMN with downtime planning.
