2198	1155	2139	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	Page splits occur when an INSERT must go into a full page. SQL Server allocates a new page and moves ~50% of rows. Buffer pool impact: (1) the original page and new page are both dirty, (2) the split temporarily holds exclusive latches on both pages, (3) fragmentation from splits reduces readahead efficiency. NEWID() as clustered key is the classic cause — every insert targets a random position. Fix: use NEWSEQUENTIALID() instead of NEWID(), use IDENTITY columns for clustered keys, set fillfactor < 100 on indexes with random insert patterns. Page Splits/sec includes benign splits from new page allocations — cross-reference with leaf_allocation_count in dm_db_index_operational_stats for actual mid-page splits.
