2203	1157	2144	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	Temp tables (#temp, ##temp) and table variables (@table) live in tempdb. Their data and index pages compete for buffer pool space with user database pages. Heavy temp table usage patterns: (1) ETL staging in temp tables, (2) cursor-based processing with temp storage, (3) stored procedures creating large temp tables per execution, (4) massive INSERT...SELECT into #temp for multi-step processing. Since tempdb is database_id 2, you can measure its buffer pool share directly via dm_os_buffer_descriptors. Fix: reduce temp table sizes, use CTEs or derived tables, rewrite cursor logic to set-based, use memory-optimized temp tables (SQL 2019+).
