2205	1158	2146	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	A single query reading 50K+ pages (400 MB+) can wipe the buffer pool working set. SQL Server 2016+ has a 'large scan' threshold where scans that exceed a fraction of the buffer pool are deprioritized (pages are placed at the cold end of the LRU list). However, this mitigation doesn't prevent the initial reads from displacing pages. Free list stalls (free_list_stalls_sec > 0) mean the lazywriter can't free pages fast enough — the buffer pool is under severe pressure. Lazy writes/sec > 20 means the lazywriter is actively evicting pages to make room. Fix: add TOP/pagination to large queries, use Resource Governor to cap memory grants, schedule large scans during off-peak hours.
