2209	1160	2150	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	SQL Server's readahead mechanism pre-fetches pages that a query is likely to need (up to 64 contiguous pages for sequential scans). While readahead improves individual query performance, it can flood the buffer pool with prefetched pages that displace actively used cached pages. The readahead decision is made per-extent and is more aggressive for sequential scan patterns. This is particularly harmful when: (1) multiple queries trigger readahead simultaneously, (2) readahead pages are never actually used (over-prediction), (3) the buffer pool is already under pressure. SQL Server does not expose a direct readahead tuning knob — the fix is to reduce the scan workload itself. Trace flag 652 disables readahead (diagnostic only, not recommended in production).
