2211	1161	2152	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	Long-running queries in SQL Server keep buffer pool pages referenced (pinned) while they iterate over result sets. While SQL Server uses a clock-based page replacement algorithm (not strict pinning), actively-referenced pages get their reference bit refreshed, making them harder to evict. Under RCSI (Read Committed Snapshot Isolation), long queries also keep version store entries alive, growing tempdb. The combination of high logical reads + long duration means the query's working set competes with all other queries for buffer pool space for the entire execution. Fix: optimize the query to run faster, add TOP/OFFSET-FETCH pagination, use OPTION(FAST n) for streaming results, or kill the offending session.
