2234	1171	2174	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	Unfiltered subqueries (SELECT * FROM table WHERE col IN (SELECT col FROM large_table)) cause the optimizer to estimate full cardinality of the inner query. This inflates Sort, Hash Match, and Spool memory grants. Fix: add WHERE clauses to subqueries, rewrite as JOINs with proper predicates, or use OPTION (MIN_GRANT_PERCENT = 0, MAX_GRANT_PERCENT = N) to cap grants. Query Store feedback (SQL Server 2022+) auto-corrects persistent grant issues.
2213	1162	2154	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	When the cardinality estimator underestimates row counts, sort and hash operators receive insufficient memory grants. At runtime, when the actual data exceeds the grant, SQL Server spills the operation to tempdb. This causes: (1) tempdb I/O (slow), (2) tempdb space growth, (3) query slowdown by 10-100x. The total_spills column in dm_exec_query_stats (SQL 2016+) directly reports spill page counts. An estimation_ratio > 10 is a strong signal. Fix: UPDATE STATISTICS, add multi-column statistics for correlated predicates, use query hints (OPTION(HASH JOIN) or OPTION(MIN_GRANT_PERCENT)), try the new CE model (TF 2312).
