2220	1165	2161	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	Parameter sniffing occurs when SQL Server compiles a plan for the first parameter value encountered, then reuses that plan for all subsequent executions. If the first value returns few rows, the memory grant is small — later executions with high-cardinality values spill. Step 1 checks for min_grant_kb = max_grant_kb (same plan reused) with high spill variance (actual needs vary dramatically). Step 2 extracts the sniffed vs runtime values from the plan XML. Fix: (1) OPTION(RECOMPILE) for variable-cardinality queries, (2) OPTION(OPTIMIZE FOR UNKNOWN) for average-based estimates, (3) Query Store with plan forcing, (4) OPTIMIZE FOR @param = typical_value for known distributions. SQL 2022+ has Parameter Sensitive Plan Optimization that generates multiple plans for different parameter ranges.
