2352	1225	2290	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	In a parallel hash join or sort, the total memory grant is divided among threads. If data skew sends 80% of rows to one thread, that thread's share of the grant is insufficient and it spills, while other threads have unused memory. This is different from RC01 (insufficient total grant) — here the total grant may be adequate, but the per-thread distribution is not. SQL Server cannot dynamically redistribute memory between parallel threads. Fix: reduce skew by rewriting the query to pre-aggregate before the hash join, use OPTION(HASH GROUP) vs OPTION(ORDER GROUP) strategically, or reduce DOP so each thread handles a larger share. In SQL Server 2019+, batch mode memory grant feedback can help over multiple executions.
