2761	1437	2697	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	Service accounts are the most common 'orphaned' accounts in SQL Server. They are created for a specific application or job, but the application is decommissioned while the account remains. Nobody knows if the account is still needed, so it stays enabled. Service accounts often have elevated permissions (db_owner, sysadmin) and non-expiring passwords. Without an account inventory documenting owner, purpose, and review schedule, these accounts become permanent attack vectors. Fix: (1) create a service account registry documenting owner, purpose, and review date, (2) implement password rotation on a schedule, (3) disable accounts not used in 90 days after confirming with the documented owner.
