2898	1507	2834	1	next	\N	ruled_out	\N	SQL Server Audit stores the statement text in a nvarchar(4000) field in file-based audit targets. DDL statements longer than 4000 characters are silently truncated — no error, no warning. This affects: (1) CREATE TABLE with many columns (common in data warehouses), (2) ALTER TABLE with multiple column additions, (3) CREATE/ALTER PROCEDURE with large bodies, (4) dynamic DDL generated by ORMs or migration tools. The truncation means forensic analysis cannot reconstruct what was actually changed. Fix: (1) supplement SQL Server Audit with DDL triggers that capture the full EVENTDATA() XML (no truncation), (2) use Extended Events with large_data_support for full statement capture, (3) require DDL to be committed through version-controlled migration scripts rather than ad-hoc execution.
